'''You receive a non-clickable [[Burning Soul of the Pious]]'''. 

Murder the living version of [[Lord Grimrot]]. You will get the usual faction hits from killing him.

Loot the [[Burning Soul of the Pestilent]] and the [[Pestilence Scythe]] with 1 charge of [[Vampiric Curse]] and no item lore. (There is another [[Pestilence Scythe]] of the same name with no charges that isn't required for this quest. Its item lore says "Pestilence Scythe(No Soul)".)

Right-click the [[Burning Soul of the Pestilent]]: 

: As you stare into the fading embers within your palm, the feeling of redemption is lost upon you as you bear witness to the visage of Lord Grimrot cursing you from beyond for your sudden betrayal. Within moments you hear laughter in your mind as you are pierced by an overencumbrance of terror that speaks to your very being. "This loathsome soul sought to serve my enemy and deserved such a fate. As for your pitiful plight, if you still live in fear of your regrets, then seek out Jotayi Ibupran. Show them you have served me well, and you may yet prove your worth."

The next part requires [[Heretics]] Warmly Faction.
* You can gain [[Heretics]] faction by killing Kobolds in [[The Warrens]]. At +5 [[Heretics]] per Kobold kill, it takes about ~2 rounds of killing the entire zone. 
* If you are a Paladin, it helps to set your diety to Agnostic. 

Go to [[Paineel]] and find [[Jotayi Ibupran]] (726, 856, -54) in the Necromancer guild, The Abattoir, on the third-floor balcony overlooking the courtyard.

Turn in the [[Burning Soul of the Pestilent]] and the [[Pestilence Scythe]] (with charges, not uncharged) to Jotayi Ibupran.

: Jotayi Ibupran says, 'My master has sent you? Such is the will of Cazic-Thule. I beseech you to be my vengeance. Before the war, I was friend to a paladin named Inte Akera, a man who could not let go of his sense of zeal. In our last battle, I fell to his blade, leaving me now the grim image you see before you. I believe he must have left Norrath some time ago, as I can no longer sense his life force anymore. It would bring my soul to rest to see his face beyond the veil.'

